Combined strategies: A good strategy is to pick up every
dagger encountered, wield one for whatever melee can't be
avoided, and throw all of the others to increase your dagger
skill as fast as possible.  Switch to offensive spells when
your daggers run out or by choice to #enhance spell skills.
Only use Sting in melee when forced.

Here's the bad part if you don't #name Sting - Daggers stack
so you have to #name the one you wield anyways.  Here's the
good part if you #name Sting - Sting doesn't stack so once
you've #named it you can wield it and never worry about
throwing any non-wielded dagger; you won't end up wielding a
stack.

Wizards can become Expert at dagger, quarterstaff, several
junk weapon classes, and they have all spell skills available
to at least Skilled.  The skill slots in the several weapons
classes are put there for distraction.  Unless you plan on
wishing for the healer's Quest staff I think the quaterstaff
slots are put there as a distraction as well.  Go for dagger
and spells and don't advance any other skills.  Unless you
are doing a challenge game like a Harry Potter squib.

And if you end up getting one of the super-artifacts later
in the game, go ahead and advance it to Basic and use it.
Many wizards are chaotic and chaotics get Stormbringer as a
crowning gift, may as well use that and no longer settle for
Sting after that point in the game.

